Evolutionary Mission Trajectory Generator (EMTG)
Community fork — version 9.2.0 release candidate

Originating author: Jacob Englander
Fork maintenance: Keystone Intelligence and community contributors
NASA upstream: https://github.com/nasa/EMTG
Fork issues and support: https://github.com/keystoneintelligence/EMTG/issues
License: NASA Open Source Agreement; see EMTG_NOSA_License.pdf

This community fork builds on NASA GSFC's EMTG. NASA authorship and license
notices are retained; NASA does not maintain or endorse this fork.
Fresh-machine qualification of this public candidate remains pending.

The primary release target is the Windows x64 IPOPT-only command-line solver.
Ubuntu 22.04 x64 packaging is experimental. Provisioned Windows and Ubuntu
container builds have passed development CTest, bounded native IPOPT,
dependency, relocation, and release-path checks. These results do not qualify
every mission, every platform, or all historical NASA functionality.

The managed dependency graph uses IPOPT 3.14.11, MUMPS, reference LAPACK,
OpenBLAS, Boost, and CSPICE. The separate Linux solver CI graph uses IPOPT
3.14.19 and needs its own evidence. Public artifacts exclude SNOPT. Licensed
users can select SNOPT explicitly in an appropriately configured source build.

IPOPT is the default solver (NLP_solver_type 2); SNOPT is 0. Existing files that
omitted NASA's old SNOPT default now select IPOPT. Native scientific formats
are retained, general feasibility defaults to 1e-5, and fixed-step integration
remains the default. Adaptive integration is experimental. A different solver
can converge to a different solution, so compare physical acceptance envelopes.

There is no qualified macOS build or binary. Propulator, PyHardware, and the
historical wxPython GUI are optional developer workflows with separate
qualification requirements; they are not covered by the managed CLI release.
Native result parsing through PyEMTG.Results works independently of the GUI.
Optional OuterLoop search remains available. Applications own translation and
publication policy.

After installing the documented prerequisites, use build.ps1 on Windows or
build.sh --bootstrap on Ubuntu. Portable bundles discover adjacent runtime data;
large SPICE BSP kernels remain external. Use short Windows checkout and output
paths. The legacy PDF build guides are historical references.

See README.md, SUPPORT.md, BUILDING.md, INSTALLING.md, and CONTRIBUTING.md in
the source checkout for current instructions and support limits. Source/build
availability does not imply that a qualified release has been published. Use
this fork's GitHub issues for fork-specific support; maintainer response times
are not guaranteed. Please credit NASA's original EMTG work when reporting
results. Included dependencies retain their own notices and licenses; see
THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md and the notices distributed with each artifact.

